#: ../../../crypto/libcrypto-zeroization.rst:4
msgid "Crypto Key Zeroization"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../crypto/libcrypto-zeroization.rst:6
msgid ""
"This document describes the conventions for zeroizing crypto structures in "
"the kernel."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../crypto/libcrypto-zeroization.rst:9
msgid ""
"Note: the kernel follows traditional cryptographic terminology by using the "
"term \"zeroizing\" to mean erasing sensitive parameters to prevent their "
"disclosure if the system is later compromised.  This distinguishes it from "
"zeroing memory for other purposes such as initialization."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../crypto/libcrypto-zeroization.rst:14
msgid "Contents"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../crypto/libcrypto-zeroization.rst:17
msgid "Overview"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../crypto/libcrypto-zeroization.rst:19
msgid ""
"Cryptographic key material and intermediate state (such as HMAC contexts) "
"must be zeroized after use to prevent sensitive data from lingering on the "
"stack or heap, where it could be leaked through memory disclosure "
"vulnerabilities, crash dumps, or cold-boot attacks."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../crypto/libcrypto-zeroization.rst:24
msgid ""
"For memory that has been allocated with kmalloc() or a similar function, "
"kfree_sensitive() should be used instead of kfree() to release the memory."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../crypto/libcrypto-zeroization.rst:27
msgid ""
"For other cases, the kernel provides memzero_explicit() for clearing the "
"memory.  Unlike plain memset(), memzero_explicit() is guaranteed not to be "
"optimized away by the compiler, even when the memory being cleared appears "
"to be dead."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../crypto/libcrypto-zeroization.rst:32
msgid ""
"The crypto library builds on memzero_explicit() by providing typed "
"zeroization helpers for each key and context structure.  These helpers serve "
"two purposes:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../crypto/libcrypto-zeroization.rst:36
msgid ""
"They make __cleanup() annotations possible, so that structures on the stack "
"are automatically zeroized when they go out of scope."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../crypto/libcrypto-zeroization.rst:39
msgid ""
"They improve readability by replacing ``memzero_explicit(&key, "
"sizeof(key))`` with a self-documenting call like ``aes_zeroize_key(&key)``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../crypto/libcrypto-zeroization.rst:44
msgid "What to zeroize"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../crypto/libcrypto-zeroization.rst:46
msgid ""
"The following types of structures hold sensitive material and should be "
"zeroized after use:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../crypto/libcrypto-zeroization.rst:49
msgid ""
"**Key structures** (e.g. ``struct aes_key``, ``struct hmac_sha256_key``): "
"contain expanded round keys or prepared key material."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../crypto/libcrypto-zeroization.rst:52
msgid ""
"**HMAC/MAC context structures** (e.g. ``struct hmac_sha256_ctx``, ``struct "
"aes_cmac_ctx``): contain inner and outer hash states derived from the key."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../crypto/libcrypto-zeroization.rst:56
msgid ""
"**Hash context structures** (e.g. ``struct sha256_ctx``): may contain "
"sensitive data being hashed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../crypto/libcrypto-zeroization.rst:59
msgid ""
"Not all of these require explicit cleanup by callers.  Many ``..._final()`` "
"functions already zeroize their context internally (see `Automatic vs. "
"manual zeroization`_ below)."
msgstr ""

